course: dictionary
lesson: 4283
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: One word can have several meanings, and the words around it tell you which one you need. «The apples on the tree are still green.» Here green means not ripe yet, because the apples are not ready to pick.
explain: «We played on the village green.» A village green is a grassy area, not a colour. The word village tells you where, and the grass is the clue.
explain: «Ireland is the green island to the west of Great Britain; its frequent rain keeps the grass green all year.» The rain helps you see that green means covered in grass, and the colour is the grass's colour.
explain: Ask yourself: what word would fit here? Then put your meaning into the sentence and check that it still makes sense.
fact: Green is the colour of grass and leaves.
fact: Green can mean not ripe yet, as in «green bananas».
fact: Green can mean good for nature, as in «green energy».
fact: Greens are green vegetables, and «eat your greens» means eat your green vegetables.
fact: Plants are green because of a substance called chlorophyll, and blue and yellow paint mixed together make green.
exercise: «The apples on the tree are still green.» What does green mean here? | *Not ripe yet | Full of envy | Good for nature | A grassy area to play on
exercise: «We played football on the village green.» What is a village green? | A kind of sea | A traffic light | *A grassy area in the village | A type of bean
exercise: «Eat your greens» means what? | Paint the walls green | Run around the field | *Eat your green vegetables | Stop and wait for a minute
test: «Green light» means what? | Turn around | Wait | *Go | Stop
test: The word green is used in «green energy». What does it mean here? | *Good for nature | Jealous | Not ripe yet | The colour of the sky
exam: Blue and yellow mixed together make which colour? | *Green | Orange | Brown | Purple
